# REL-108 isolated timer success qualification

Run after the ARC 000058 migration acceptance has completed. Update the
isolated worktree to the selected main revision. The second argument is
the full commit SHA containing the accepted ARC migration. The wrapper
verifies ancestry, the exact up and down migration files, and a clean
checkout. It builds the Linux amd64 inputs
from that exact checkout and records the source and binary digests.

```sh
bash resume.sh "$ownsolid_worktree" "$accepted_arc_commit"
```

The host must already contain the digest-pinned PostgreSQL 18.6 and
pgvector 0.8.7 image referenced by the wrapper. The combined run requires
Linux amd64, systemd, Docker Compose, TUN, at least 4 GiB available
memory and 20 GiB free disk, and full I/O pressure at most 20 percent
over the previous ten seconds. The fixture checks resources before and
after taking the shared lock. Exit 75 denotes a busy lock or a resource
refusal. No container or unit is started in that case.

The runner uses the existing installer materialization path, the real
Control migration and the shipped service and timer under private
names. It starts only one capped PostgreSQL container, with a tmpfs
data volume, no published ports and no external network. A native
egress recorder is the only IPv4 and IPv6 default route.

The four actual timer outcomes must be succeeded, succeeded, failed,
succeeded. Every row must confirm verified publication. Successful
expiry counts must be zero, one and two, in order. The failed expiry
must have code INSTALL_BACKUP_RETENTION_FAILED and omit the count.
The injected owner link belongs to the fixture. It is removed before
the retry. The failed publication remains readable, and its history
survives expiry. Each publication is independently loaded into a
scratch database and compared with the current deployment, schema and
table count. The live OID and ordinary backup sentinel must survive.
The capture must contain only the harness's IPv4, IPv6 and DNS probes.

The wrapper stores the input hashes and Linux transcript under logs.
It removes only its own container, runtime units and scratch files.
